/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:197:  Deviation candidate #21: **splitIntegerTraces per-peak m/z**: It builds Peak2D with per-peak m/z recovered from bin + per-frame factor — wait, the prompt's question 3 says "builds MassTrace objects with every peak at the SAME m/z (t.mz)" — but the code sets `q.setMZ(bi != end ? ax.mzOf(t.xt[i], bi->second) : t.mz)`. So actually per-peak m/z IS recovered (with per-frame calibration via b_of_rt). The prompt's claim in Q3 seems slightly off — unless `have_bin` fails. `have_bin = ax.ok && i < t.xt.size()`. t.xt was filled in detectTracesInteger_. So mostly per-peak m/z. Hmm wait — but toTrace after splitting: t.mz = whatever toTrace reports under trace:mz_estimator. Then `t.tof = ax.tofOf(t.mz, t.b)` — the re-attached bin is computed from the REPORTED mz (e.g. mean/median of per-peak m/z) inverted with the APEX frame's factor. If the estimator reports a weighted mean m/z that differs from any actual peak bin, tofOf gives the nearest bin — "the store is ~400x finer than a bin, so the inversion recovers the measured bin exactly" — hmm, that claim: bins are integers of a fine store; mzOf then tofOf round trip recovers the same bin if quantization is fine. But t.mz from toTrace under, say, "median" estimator equals one of the per-peak m/z values → inversion with the apex frame's factor gives the bin that that m/z would have IN THE APEX FRAME, which may differ from the bin in its own frame if per-frame calibration drifted (b differs per frame!). That's the bug seed in Q2: re-attached bin uses apex frame's b, but the m/z came from a peak in a DIFFERENT frame; with per-frame calibration differences, tofOf(mz, b_apex) ≠ the original measured bin. Impact: downstream extraction in bin space could be off by drift amount. COORD mostly.
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:201:  Also EPD and per-peak m/z: ElutionPeakDetection operates on chromatographic profile (RT/intensity); m/z used only for reporting centroids (filterByMassTraceSNR? disabled). So per-peak m/z fidelity doesn't affect splitting decisions — only reported coordinates. COORD. Losing per-peak m/z would change "mean"/"median" estimators: toTrace under "mean" uses per-peak m/z → with recovered per-peak m/z it's near-correct; if all same (fallback), mean = t.mz anyway.
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:277:  **Q3**: Does EPD care about per-peak m/z? ElutionPeakDetection splits on the RT/intensity chromatogram; m/z per peak is used only for centroid recomputation (and masstrace S/N filter, disabled). So splitting decisions unaffected. The code actually recovers per-peak m/z (mzOf per frame) — contradicting the question's premise; the fallback (t.mz for all) only when bin missing. toTrace afterwards: under estimator "mean"/"median", per-peak m/z matters → recovered values make it nearly right; if the flat fallback path were taken, mean/median would collapse to the weighted centroid m/z — COORD only.
