/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:672:  M. **EPD behavior**: ElutionPeakDetection on MassTrace uses convex hulls of the chromatogram (RT/intensity); per-peak m/z irrelevant to splitting (used only for reporting + optional filters which are off). Q3's premise (all peaks same m/z) is not what the code does — per-peak m/z IS recovered. If it were flat, splitting still identical; only reported centroids after toTrace would collapse to t.mz for mean/median estimators. COORD only, never SET.
/Users/kohlbach/Claude/speXtract/docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:694:  U. **`if (split_valleys <= 0.0 || in.size() < 2) return std::move(in);`** — comment "OpenMS branch: EPD needs > 1". Actually OpenMS calls EPD regardless of trace count; EPD on a single trace still filters (e.g. min length?). EPD's filterByPeakWidth is off, masstrace_snr off — with everything off, EPD on 1 trace returns it unchanged modulo... EPD always recomputes centroids? ElutionPeakDetection::detectPeaks: if chrom_fwhm split... it does smoothing/SNR per trace. With width filtering off and snr off, a single trace passes through. So skipping EPD when in.size() < 2 is behaviorally fine. Minor deviation: EPD ALSO would run when split_valleys <= 0 in OpenMS (EPD always runs in the FTFF pipeline after MTD). The tool's early return skips EPD entirely if split_valleys ≤ 0 — but the setup says split_valleys = chrom_fwhm > 0, so fine. Flag as fragility.
