docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:9:The window loop alone consumes 77,333 CPU-s = 21.48 CPU-h—2.94× diaTracer’s entire 7.3 CPU-h. Even deleting every other speXtract phase would not produce CPU parity. Scheduler improvements can plausibly remove 15–25% wall time. Closing the remaining gap requires reducing the number of fragment traces and/or precursor–fragment correlation work.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:32:| Vacant outer slots | 10.25 | \(96 × (1-7.146/8)\): tail, admission stalls, or gaps between stages |
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:134:Fix the MS1 serial preparation eventually, especially for sensitivity settings that explode the trace count. Do not mistake it for the competitive gap.
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:140:Several algorithmic differences can plausibly produce a threefold intermediate-work gap:
docs/reviews/window-loop-perf-2026-09-03/codex.md:154:The honest conclusion is that scheduler tuning alone cannot close the gap. At an impossible perfect 96-core loop speedup, the measured loop still takes \(77333/96 = 806\) s. Adding the other phases gives roughly 16:58—still 1.74× diaTracer.
