docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.md:17:  1. **Termination rule.** OpenMS default is `trace_termination_criterion="outlier"`: stop a direction after **5 consecutive** misses (`MassTraceDetection.cpp:41-43`, applied at 577/630). The per-direction `hits/visited < min_sample_rate` rule in §3 and in the draft (DIAPseudoSpectra.cpp:825) is OpenMS's *sample_rate* mode, which the tool does not run — and even that mode OpenMS implements with a 5-scan grace period and a **combined both-directions** hit/scans denominator (MassTraceDetection.cpp:582-593), not the draft's per-direction counter. Three different rules; the draft implements a fourth.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.md:18:  2. **The final quality gate is missing entirely.** OpenMS *also* applies `min_sample_rate` at acceptance: `isTraceValid_` (MassTraceDetection.cpp:445-469) computes `trace.size() / (total_scans − trailing_consecutive_misses)` and rejects below threshold. The draft has no post-hoc quality check at all — it emits whatever survived extension. This is the project's "sole surviving MS2 trace-validity lever" (your own comment, line 1261, with a measured peptide loss when mis-set), and the draft silently deletes it.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.md:40:  **Cheap detection on real data:** (a) one SQLite query — `NumScans` from `analysis.tdf` (SQLiteCpp is already the `loadTofAxis` dependency, line 492) — against a per-frame count of distinct `imq`; if distinct ≈ peak count or ≫ NumScans, the premise is dead. (b) Grid test: for true scan-valued data, gaps between consecutive distinct values are multiples of a common per-frame quantum; picked centroids fail this immediately. (c) Cross-frame stability: histogram the same trace's member `imq` across frames — if stable, ranking buys nothing over gating on `imq` directly.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.md:49:  - **A large share of t_trace is ElutionPeakDetection, which the integer path currently skips.** `detectTraces_` runs `do_split` inside the timed region (lines 1378-1390, 1493 ff.); your own comment at 1485 records EPD-off saving ~28% wall (20:46 vs 28:40). The integer branch (2962-2993) never calls EPD, and there is no EPD adapter for `Trace`. So as drafted, `detector=integer` will show a t_trace drop of which a large fraction is *silently skipping valley splitting* — the exact merged-trace regression your comments at 1478-1484 and the +36.7%-peptides MS1 result (line 1257) document. A speedup that is mostly an unmeasured correctness cut.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-2026-09-03/kimi.md:60:  3. **`min_sample_rate` semantics (design #1) — worse than stated.** It's not "subtly different": different termination rule, missing final quality gate, different denominators (§1.1-1.2). Symptom: gappy fragment traces vanish or survive differently; the measured 8,865 → 8,735 sensitivity (line 1261) says this lever moves peptides at the percent level.
