docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:54:  Key deviation candidate #1: **The `total` in isTraceValid_**. OpenMS: `isTraceValid_(trace, total_scans_visited, missed_down, missed_up)` where `adjusted = total_scans_visited - missed_down - missed_up; return trace.size()/adjusted >= min_sample_rate`. What is total_scans_visited in OpenMS? In run_: there's a counter... Actually in OpenMS, after the loop:
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:135:  Deviation candidate: `adjusted` guard. OpenMS: `adjusted = total - missed_down - missed_up; return trace.size()/adjusted >= min_sample_rate;` — no guard against adjusted <= 0 (division by zero → inf → passes?). Tool: `adjusted = total > dn.missed + up.missed ? total - ... : 1`. Hmm, if adjusted is 0 in OpenMS, size/0 = inf (integer division? trace.size() is Size (unsigned); `trace.size()/adjusted` — in OpenMS isTraceValid_: `return static_cast<double>(trace.size())/static_cast<double>(adjusted) >= min_sample_rate;`? I believe there's a cast. If adjusted == 0 → inf >= msr → true. Tool would use 1 → size/1 ≥ msr also likely true. Marginal; also can missed exceed total? missed counts increments only when frame_live and no acceptable peak; scans counts every step. missed ≤ scans per direction, and total = scans+1, so missed_down+missed_up ≤ scans_total < total. So adjusted ≥ 1 always; the guard is dead code. Fine, no deviation.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:137:  Deviation candidate #2: **min_trace_length**. OpenMS default min_trace_length is 0.0? No wait — in OpenMS MassTraceDetection, min_trace_length default is 0.0? Actually I recall default min_trace_length = 0.0 and max = -1.0 (disabled). The prompt says "min_trace_length / max_trace_length from the caller". Tool: `if (max_len_sec > 0.0 && span_s > max_len_sec) continue;` with comment "the tool passes 0 for off". OpenMS: `if (max_len >= 0 && rt_range > max_len) -> false`. If max_len is exactly 0.0: OpenMS treats 0 >= 0 as enabled → any span > 0 fails. Tool: 0 → off. Deviation only if caller passes exactly 0.0 meaning "enabled 0" — unlikely but a boundary difference. Minor. Actually also `min_sample_rate`: msr = min_sample_rate >= 0 ? : 0.5 — OpenMS default is 0.5, fine.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:139:  Deviation candidate #3: **min_sample_rate comparison operator**. OpenMS isTraceValid_: `return (double)trace.size() / (double)adjusted >= min_sample_rate;`. Tool: `if ((double)got.size() / (double)adjusted < msr) continue;` — equivalent to >=. ✓.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:159:  Deviation candidate #8: **hits counter unused** — OpenMS uses hits? In OpenMS, `if (peaks_down + peaks_up >= min_trace_length...)`? No — actually, hmm. OpenMS's isTraceValid_ doesn't use hits. But termination: `trace_termination_criterion "outlier"` uses missed. There's also "sample_rate" criterion: `if (SAMPLE_RATE && (double)hits/scans < min_sample_rate) active=false`. Since criterion is "outlier" only, hits unused. Tool matches for the given config but doesn't implement sample_rate termination — fine per defaults stated.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:195:  Deviation candidate #20: **traces pushed even when got.size()==1?** OpenMS: min_trace_length default 0.0, min_sample_rate 0.5. A single-peak trace: span = 0 >= min_len (if min_len 0), total=1, adjusted=1, size/adjusted = 1 ≥ 0.5 → valid! OpenMS would emit single-peak traces too (if min_len=0). Then EPD requires ≥... split_valleys with length 1: `in.size() < 2` check is about number of TRACES not peaks. Hmm, EPD on single-peak traces — MassTrace with 1 point; EPD filter might drop. Same both sides presumably. Tool matches. But wait — the tool's early return `if (split_valleys <= 0.0 || in.size() < 2) return std::move(in);` — "OpenMS branch: EPD needs > 1" — hmm, this means if fewer than 2 traces, no splitting. Fine, trivial.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:263:  Hmm, or does isTraceValid_ do the +1 internally? The prompt's excerpt: `adjusted = total_scans_visited - missed_down - missed_up; return trace.size()/adjusted >= min_sample_rate;` — and the tool's `total = dn.scans + up.scans + 1`. My best recollection supports scan counters + 1 (the seed scan). I'll state it as: OpenMS passes scan_counter_down + scan_counter_up + 1 (the +1 accounts for the apex spectrum). Tool matches.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:265:  One more check on the sample-rate semantics: adjusted = scans visited where the spectrum was NON-EMPTY minus... wait: total - missed. total counts every visited scan including empty ones; missed counts non-empty-but-rejected. So adjusted = empty-scan count + hit count. trace.size()/adjusted — hits/(hits+empties). Sample rate = fraction of non-... it's hits / (hits + empty scans). Interesting: empty scans count against sample rate (they're in adjusted denominator but not numerator... wait: adjusted = total - missed = (hits + misses + empties) + 1 - misses = hits + empties + 1. trace.size() = hits + 1 (apex). So ratio = (hits+1)/(hits+empties+1). Both sides same. ✓.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:269:  **Another one: `if (OUTLIER && ...)` — tool always applies outlier termination (hardcodes "outlier" criterion). Config default is "outlier" — fine, but if the config ever selects "sample_rate", tool diverges. Note as hardcoding, matches stated config.**
