docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi.err:626:  MassTrace constructor computes centroids from the peaks (updateWeightedMeanMZ etc. in MassTrace constructor from convex hull / peaks). So the reported centroid m/z in OpenMS is the trace's own weighted centroid computed by MassTrace from member peaks, NOT the running centroid — though they should be nearly identical since the running centroid is the intensity-weighted mean of the same peaks (with the apex double-counted!). The running centroid double-counts the apex; MassTrace's centroid counts each peak once. Tiny difference, COORD. The tool reports `t.mz = cmz` (line 864) — the running centroid with apex double-counted. But then splitIntegerTraces → toTrace re-estimates under trace:mz_estimator anyway (apex/mean/median), so the reported m/z after EPD comes from the MassTrace peaks, where every peak has m/z = t.mz (the running centroid!). Section 3 territory: all peaks get the same m/z = running centroid, so "apex" estimator returns the running centroid (they're all equal), "mean" returns the same, "median" same. So under the integer path the estimator choice is a no-op! Whereas the OpenMS path's estimator actually varies. That's a real COORD/SET effect downstream (fragment assignment gates on trace m/z; precursors use mono bin). For MS2 fragments, t.mz gates fragment-precursor assignment in m/z. Since EPD in the integer path gets flat m/z, `toTrace` under "apex" (default) returns the flat value — the OpenMS path returns the apex peak's actual m/z. Difference: running centroid vs apex peak m/z — small (sub-ppm typically? no — within ±3sd window, could be up to ~20 ppm at 3σ? No: apex m/z vs weighted centroid, usually within a few ppm). COORD, small.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi.err:649:  **D12. Section 3: valley splitting with flat m/z.** As analyzed: EPD splitting decision uses only RT/intensity → unaffected. But after splitting, each sub-trace's reported m/z via toTrace = flat centroid regardless of estimator. In the OpenMS path, the sub-trace's reported m/z under "apex" is the apex peak's actual m/z of that sub-trace. Also, EPD's `computeFwhm` etc. store fwhm; downstream? The tool's toTrace uses mt.getCentroidRT etc. One more: EPD may drop sub-traces? With width_filtering off and snr filtering false, detectPeaks splits but keeps all? There's a parameter `chrom_fwhm` = expected fwhm for smoothing; smoothing could merge/split differently but m/z-independent. ✓. Also MassTrace::updateWeightedMeanMZ — with flat m/z, centroid = flat value. And does EPD use `mt.getCentroidMZ()` in any filter? masstrace_snr_filtering false → no SNR filter. OK.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi.err:659:  **D13. t.rt**: integer path sets t.rt = apex RT (line 865); OpenMS path toTrace sets t.rt = mt.getCentroidRT() (intensity-weighted). Downstream gates (delta_rt) use it. COORD difference between paths: apex vs centroid RT. Small. But wait — after splitIntegerTraces, toTrace is used so t.rt = centroid RT. ✓ consistent then. When split_valleys=0 (splitIntegerTraces early-returns!), the integer traces keep t.rt=apex-RT, t.mz=running centroid, and t.tof = apex bin (set at line 863, t.tof = sl.tof[kap] — the ACTUAL measured bin of the apex peak — good). Note inconsistency: with splitting, t.tof = tofOf(centroid); without, t.tof = measured apex bin. Both paths fine individually.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi.err:663:  **D14. `t.im`**: integer path = running IM centroid; OpenMS path toTrace = mt.getCentroidIM() which was set to centroid_im in run_ — same semantics. But after EPD: MassTrace split — does the sub-trace keep centroid IM? EPD's splitting creates sub-MassTraces... setCentroidIM is a metainfo? MassTrace stores centroid_im_ as a double member? In OpenMS, MassTrace has centroid_im as a private member with setCentroidIM/getCentroidIM — when EPD creates sub-traces (it builds new MassTrace from subsets of peaks), does it copy centroid_im? EPD::detectPeaks → filterByPeakWidth / split ... In ElutionPeakDetection, sub-traces are created via `MassTrace subtrace(...)`? It slices peaks: `MassTrace(*iter, from, to)`? If it constructs new MassTraces from peak subsets, centroid_im defaults to -1? Hmm. Actually OpenMS MassTrace has `centroid_im` stored as a regular member? I recall setCentroidIM exists and EPD... this affects BOTH paths equally (the OpenMS path also goes through EPD via do_split). So not a differential. Skip.
docs/reviews/integer-tracing-v2-2026-09-03/kimi-retry.err:662:  I. **`t.mz = cmz` (L168)**: OpenMS MassTrace's centroid m/z — what does OpenMS report? MassTrace computes its own centroid as intensity-weighted mean of gathered peak m/z's? Actually MassTrace::updateWeightedMeanMZ uses the peaks; MTD sets the trace from gathered peaks; the reported centroid MZ is computed by MassTrace (weighted by intensity by default, trace_mz_estimator... hmm, MassTrace has computeCentroid... In MTD run_, after validity: `MassTrace new_trace(gathered_trace); new_trace.updateWeightedMeanRT(); ...` Actually the centroid m/z of a MassTrace is computed from its peaks (`updateWeightedMeanMZ`), NOT the running iterative centroid cmz. The iterative cmz in MTD is only used during detection! The reported MassTrace centroid_mz_ = intensity-weighted mean of the gathered peaks' m/z values. The tool sets t.mz = cmz (the iterative running centroid). ARE THEY EQUAL? The iterative weighted mean (with the double-counted seed and the multiplicative update) — let's check: after seed double application, cnt = 2 I0 m0, den = 2 I0. After peaks 1..n: cnt = 2 I0 m0 + Σ Ii mi, den = 2 I0 + Σ Ii. The running centroid c satisfies c = cnt/den? Check the update: c *= (1 + w v/cnt)/(1 + w/den) → new c = c * (cnt + w v)/cnt * den/(den + w) = (cnt/den) * (cnt + wv)/(den + w)... since c = cnt/den inductively: new c = (cnt + wv)/(den + w) = new cnt/new den. ✓ So cmz = (2 I0 m0 + Σ Ii mi)/(2 I0 + Σ Ii) — the intensity-weighted mean with the seed counted TWICE. MassTrace::updateWeightedMeanMZ gives (I0 m0 + Σ Ii mi)/(I0 + Σ Ii). DIFFERENT — the tool's reported t.mz is the weighted mean with the apex double-weighted! COORD difference (small, since apex is usually the dominant peak, pulling reported m/z slightly toward apex). This is a real, if small, systematic difference from OpenMS's reported centroid. Note: in splitIntegerTraces, per-peak m/z values are set on the MassTrace and toTrace() under trace:mz_estimator recomputes — so after splitting, t.mz comes from toTrace, not cmz. But for the UNSPLIT path (split_valleys ≤ 0 → early return), t.mz = cmz stands. And EPD is always run per the setup... split_valleys>0 given (chrom_fwhm = split_valleys). OK so mostly washed out by toTrace. Still flag L168 as COORD for any consumer using pre-split t.mz.
