     1	# Where the integer detector's memory goes
     2	
     3	**Method.** A byte ledger over the window loop's data structures (`SPEXTRACT_MEM_LEDGER=1`,
     4	`MemGuard` RAII around each allocation), recording PEAK CONCURRENT bytes per structure rather than a
     5	sum over time -- windows and their band tasks run at once, so simultaneity is the whole question.
     6	Measured on S30, 100 threads, 128-core node, `trace:detector=integer`. Process peak RSS 105 GB.
     7	
     8	The first ledger run over-reported traces at 63.5 GB because `buildFragGrid` consumes every trace
     9	profile and nothing subtracted it, making that one counter cumulative. The tell was that it exceeded
    10	the total point-bytes in the file. Corrected below.
    11	
    12	## 1. Per-structure inventory (measured)
    13	
    14	| structure | peak concurrent | bytes/element | what it is |
    15	|---|---|---|---|
    16	| **traces (`xi`/`xv`/`xt` + `Trace`)** | **35.6 GB** | 12 B/point + 120 B/trace | detected traces, from detection until the grid consumes them |
    17	| **prep (`frame_of`/`member`/`order`)** | **14.3 GB** | 4 + 1 B/peak, 4 B/seed | per-window preparation shared by the band tasks |
    18	| **slab (`tof`/`inten`/`imq`)** | **12.0 GB** | 10 B/peak | the peaks themselves |
    19	| EPD `list<Peak2D>` | 6.0 GB | ~48 B/point | valley-splitting round trip |
    20	| emitted spectra | 5.0 GB | 16 B/peak | accumulate across ALL windows until the write |
    21	| `visited` flags | 4.9 GB | 1 B/peak **per band task** | consume-and-mark state |
    22	| EPD `MassTrace` + split | 3.6 GB | ~500 B/trace, doubled | `mts` and `split` coexist |
    23	| FragGrid | 1.7 GB | 8 B/point | correlation grid |
    24	| pdense scratch | 0.02 MB | 4 B/grid slot | per-thread |
    25	| **sum of per-structure peaks** | 83.1 GB | | (not simultaneous) |
    26	| **simultaneous peak of the total** | **68.6 GB** | | ledger's own peak |
    27	| process peak RSS | 105 GB | | the 36 GB gap is allocator overhead and untracked OpenMS internals |
    28	
    29	For comparison the OpenMS path's ledger accounts only 27 GB of its 162 GB RSS, because
    30	`MassTraceDetection`'s band sub-maps and its own `MassTrace` objects are inside OpenMS and not
    31	instrumented -- its materialised `PeakMap` alone is 24.0 GB against the slab's 12.0 GB for the same
    32	peaks.
    33	
    34	## 2. Duplication, with lifetimes
    35	
    36	| pair | simultaneous? | cost | verdict |
    37	|---|---|---|---|
    38	| `TracePrep.member[P]` vs `slab.inten[P]` | yes, whole window | 1.2 GB | **pure duplication**: `member[k]` IS `inten[k] > noise`, an O(1) recompute |
    39	| `TracePrep.frame_of[P]` vs `slab.frame_off[F+1]` | yes, whole window | 4.8 GB | **derivable**: a peak's frame is a binary search over 1,400 boundaries, and every hot-path use already knows the frame |
    40	| `Trace.xt` vs `slab.tof` | yes -- the slab outlives the traces | ~10 GB | **duplication**: `xt` copies bins the slab still holds; storing the PEAK INDEX would give bin, frame and intensity |
    41	| `Trace.xv` vs `slab.inten` | yes | ~10 GB | same |
    42	| `Trace.{xi,xv,xt}` vs EPD `list<Peak2D>` | brief, per chunk | 12 vs 48 B/point | the profiles are freed as each list is built, so only one chunk's worth overlaps |
    43	| EPD `mts` vs `split` | yes, per chunk | 3.6 GB | `splitValleys` returns a new vector while the input is alive |
    44	| slab vs compact store | no | -- | `toSlab` consumes the frames as it goes |
    45	
    46	## 3. Repeated passes over the same data
    47	
    48	Per peak, from slab to spectrum: (1) `toSlab` conversion; (2) `prepareTracing` frame/member pass;
    49	(3) `prepareTracing` order-fill pass; (4) the intensity sort; (5) N candidate visits during
    50	extension. **Passes 2 and 3 are two sweeps that could be one.** Per trace point: written once by the
    51	detector, read once into a `list<Peak2D>`, read once back out of the split `MassTrace`, read once by
    52	`buildFragGrid` -- four touches of data that is 12 bytes wide, three of them only because valley
    53	splitting round-trips through OpenMS objects.
    54	
    55	## 4. `visited`: the clearest single defect
    56	
    57	Each band task allocates `vector<char> visited(P)` at the WHOLE window's peak count, although it
    58	seeds only within its own twelfth of the flight-time range. Measured 4.9 GB concurrent; the
    59	reviewers' static estimate is 14.4 GB at full concurrency. As a bitset it is 8x smaller. **And a
    60	per-band `visited` is not merely wasteful, it is wrong**: two bands can each claim the same
    61	out-of-core peak, because extension is unrestricted while the flag is not shared. One shared
    62	per-window bitset is both 12x smaller and closer to the single-threaded semantics it reimplements --
    63	at the cost of atomics on the mark.
    64	
    65	## 5. Proposed changes, ranked by measured bytes per unit of risk
    66	
    67	| # | change | saving | output | risk |
    68	|---|---|---|---|---|
    69	| 1 | `member` -> inline `inten[k] > noise` | 1.2 GB | none | none |
    70	| 2 | drop `frame_of`: carry the frame in `got` (the loop already knows it) and binary-search the two places that do not | 4.8 GB | none | none |
    71	| 3 | `visited` -> shared per-window bitset with atomic mark | 4.3 GB, and removes the cross-band double-claim | changes output (fixes a real defect) | low, needs both engines |
    72	| 4 | `Trace` stores the PEAK INDEX instead of `xi`+`xt` | ~10 GB | none | low: the slab must outlive the traces, which it already does |
    73	| 5 | one arena allocation per trace instead of three vectors | ~11 GB of allocator headers, and 3e7 small allocations become 1e7 | none | medium |
    74	| 6 | valley splitting without the `list<Peak2D>` round trip | up to 9.6 GB | none if the split is identical | high: it is OpenMS's own step |
    75	
    76	Items 1, 2 and 4 are output-neutral by construction and together are ~16 GB of the 68.6 GB ledger
    77	peak. Item 3 is the one worth doing for correctness rather than for bytes.
    78	
    79	**What this does not explain.** 105 GB RSS against a 68.6 GB ledger peak. Some is allocator
    80	fragmentation from ~3e7 small vector allocations per window (item 5 attacks it directly); some is
    81	OpenMS internals inside `ElutionPeakDetection`. Before spending effort on item 5 the right
    82	measurement is `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX` or a different allocator, which is a one-run experiment and was
    83	already falsified once for speed on this codebase -- but never tested for peak RSS.

